findings.body:
**Issue:** `body.accountId` is trusted and used as the DB lookup key with no authorization check. Any authenticated caller can pass an arbitrary `accountId` and read that account; previously lookup was hard-bound to `caller.accountId`.

**Failure mode:** `POST/PUT` (or any method that parses JSON) with `{"accountId":"victim-id"}` and a valid session returns the victim account payload — classic IDOR / broken object-level authorization.

**Fix:** Only honor a client-supplied id after an explicit staff/privilege check; otherwise force `caller.accountId` (and 403 if a non-staff caller tries to override).

```suggestion
  const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
  const requestedId = typeof body.accountId === "string" ? body.accountId : undefined;
  if (requestedId !== undefined && requestedId !== caller.accountId) {
    // require a real staff check from auth() — e.g. caller.isSupport === true
    if (!caller.isSupport) {
      return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
    }
  }
  const id: string = requestedId ?? caller.accountId;
  const account = await db.accounts.findUnique({ where: { id } });
```

Wire `isSupport` (or equivalent) from `auth`; do not ship the override without it.
